{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"**Arbitrage by authorized participants (APs)** keeps an ETF’s market price near its net asset value (NAV). If shares trade above NAV, APs can buy the underlying securities, create new ETF shares, and sell them at the higher market price—adding supply and narrowing the premium. If shares trade below NAV, they can buy ETF shares, redeem them for the underlying securities, and sell those assets—reducing share supply and narrowing the discount.\n\nThis mechanism is strongest when the ETF’s holdings are liquid and markets are open. Prices can diverge more during volatility, when markets for underlying assets are closed, or when trading costs and redemption constraints make arbitrage harder."}}
